.TH MOQ5\-RELAY 1 "September 7, 2026" "MOQ5 Relay" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
moq5\-relay \- deterministic Media over QUIC relay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBmoq5\-relay serve \-c\fR \fIfile\fR
\fBmoq5\-relay capacity \-c\fR \fIfile\fR
\fBmoq5\-relay help\fR [\fBserve\fR|\fBcapacity\fR]
\fBmoq5\-relay\fR {\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR}
\fBmoq5\-relay\fR {\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR}
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B moq5\-relay
runs a bounded Media over QUIC relay. Its raw QUIC listener supports MoQ
draft\-16 and draft\-18. An optional WebTransport listener can feed the same
relay runtime when the program was built with wtquic support.
.PP
When connections use different MoQ drafts, terminal codes are translated by
meaning rather than copied as numbers. A code with no reviewed meaning in the
target draft falls back to that draft's generic value.
.PP
Both operational commands read the same strict JSON configuration and perform
the same semantic and capacity preflight. Unknown configuration keys and
unrepresentable capacity requests are refused before a listener starts.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Start the configured listeners and run the relay. Readiness is reported only
after every configured listener, including the optional admin endpoint, has
started. Startup is all\-or\-nothing.
.TP
.B capacity
Validate the configuration and print the relay\-state allocation\-request
ceiling without opening a listener. The report identifies costs excluded from
that ceiling, including allocator overhead and transport\-owned memory, and
reports the admin thread's fixed stack reservation separately when applicable.
.TP
.BR help " [" serve | capacity ]
Print general or command\-specific help.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " file ", " \-\-config " " file ", " \-\-config= file
Read the relay configuration from
.IR file .
This option is required by
.B serve
and
.BR capacity .
There is no default configuration path. Repeating the option is an error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit. After
.B serve
or
.BR capacity ,
print help for that command. Help is answered from the command line alone: no
configuration is read and no transport is started.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the MOQ5 Relay and LibMoQ version and exit. This is a global option.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration format is documented in
.BR moq5\-relay.json (5).
The file is read once at startup and may be at most 65,535 bytes. Relative
certificate and key paths are resolved from the process working directory.
.SH LISTENERS
The
.B listener
configuration is the required raw QUIC listener. Its ordered
.B versions
array selects the supported MoQ drafts, with the server's most preferred draft
first. A one\-entry array retains exact\-version listener behavior; multiple
entries are offered on one listener.
.PP
An optional
.B webtransport
object adds a WebTransport listener with its own address, TLS credential,
request path, lane count, MoQ subprotocol list, HTTP/3 profile, and Origin
authorization policy with its optional allowlist. Both
listeners feed one relay runtime, allowing publishers and subscribers on
different transports to communicate. The sum of their lane counts may not
exceed 64. A build without wtquic support rejects this object rather than
ignoring it.
.PP
Every explicit Origin policy requires an Origin header.
.B allow_any_including_null
accepts the serialized value
.BR null ,
not an absent header. A native client that omits Origin is refused with HTTP
403 under that policy. Using
.B unset
on a draft\-13/14 listener removes that requirement and is an explicit operator
policy choice; it must not silently replace a required authorization policy.
Draft\-02 compatibility requires Origin and its request marker regardless.
.PP
Match local address families: a listener bound to
.B 127.0.0.1
does not listen on
.BR ::1 .
A client using
.B localhost
may select IPv6 first. Use a matching address and a certificate SAN for the
name or IP that the client verifies; do not disable certificate verification
to work around a destination mismatch.
.SH ADMIN ENDPOINT
An optional
.B admin.tcp
object enables a loopback\-only HTTP endpoint. It serves exactly three
resources, all read\-only and all
.BR GET :
.B /metrics
(the metrics exposition),
.B /api/v1/info
(the relay's frozen configuration and capacity model, as JSON) and
.B /api/v1/shards
(one coherent generation's per\-shard counters, as JSON). Any other target is
answered 404. The endpoint has no authentication and cannot be bound to a
non\-loopback address by the relay.
.PP
For
.BR /metrics ,
an absent
.B Accept
header, or an acceptable wildcard, selects OpenMetrics 1.0. Prometheus text
format 0.0.4 can be requested explicitly. The endpoint never returns a partial
metrics document as a successful scrape. An incomplete snapshot or temporary
capacity exhaustion receives
.B 503
with
.BR "Retry-After: 1" .
.PP
The endpoint owns a fixed\-size client table, bounded request storage, two
bounded response banks, and a dedicated fixed\-stack thread. It reads copied
snapshot rows rather than live relay structures. If the endpoint's owner exits
unexpectedly, the relay stops rather than continuing with a dead health
surface. Unix\-domain and remotely bound admin listeners are not supported.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
Request an orderly relay shutdown.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Request a Prometheus 0.0.4 metrics document and a route dump on standard error.
A multi\-lane relay also emits each shard's journal. Collection is asynchronous
and fail\-closed; an incoherent snapshot is reported as suppressed rather than
rendered with invented zeroes.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Write the retained trace records as JSON Lines on standard error, once per
shard.
.SH OUTPUT
Human messages are prefixed
.BR "MOQ5 Relay:" .
They are operator diagnostics, not a parsing interface. Versioned rows whose
names begin with
.B RELAY_
are the machine\-readable contract. Trace output is JSON Lines. Metrics use
Prometheus or OpenMetrics exposition as selected by the output surface.
With
.B logging.format
set to
.B json
in the configuration, the standard output of
.B serve
carries one JSON event per line (the same versioned records, each under a
.B schema
member) and every human message, the capacity ceiling included, goes to
standard error; the other commands stay text. After an output failure the
last line may be partial and no later event is written; see
.BR moq5-relay.json (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including an orderly shutdown and every help or version request.
.TP
.B 1
A listener, transport, allocation, or runtime operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line or configuration was refused.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a relay using a configuration in the current directory:
.PP
.nf
moq5\-relay serve \-\-config relay.json
.fi
.PP
Validate that configuration and inspect its bounded relay\-state allocation
ceiling without opening sockets:
.PP
.nf
moq5\-relay capacity \-c relay.json
.fi
.PP
Scrape an enabled admin endpoint using OpenMetrics:
.PP
.nf
curl \-H 'Accept: application/openmetrics\-text' \\
  http://127.0.0.1:9109/metrics
.fi
.SH SECURITY
Protect the private key named by the configuration using normal filesystem
permissions. The bundled
.B toy
authorization mode is a deterministic static policy, not a cryptographic token
verifier. The
.B insecure_skip_verify
transport setting is intended only for controlled development and test
environments.
.PP
The admin endpoint is deliberately restricted to loopback and has no
authentication. Put any deliberate remote exposure behind an independently
authenticated and access\-controlled service.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR moq5\-relay.json (5),
.BR curl (1)
